<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ndembu people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Southern Lunda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ndembu, or Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a>, are a people of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Their chiefdoms, headed by senior titles such as Kanongesha, were founded by Lunda nobles who moved south from the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwata-yamvo">Mwata Yamvo</a> in the 18th century. They became widely known through the anthropologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-turner">Victor Turner</a>'s studies of their rituals in the 1950s.</p></div>
